=== STEP 1 · replay: write a log, replay it, compare with a tolerance =====
  log: 600 segments, 9.4 MB (replay_log.jsonl)
  re-run of the same code      : 0/600 segments differ, 0 not bit-identical
  refactor (float32, reordered): 0/600 differ beyond tol, but 600 are not bit-identical (max diff 4.0e-04)
  behaviour change (add AEB)   : 7/600 segments differ -> open them: [52, 58, 156, 214, 324]
  'fastest' replay             : 0 late, 0 dropped frames (deadlines do not exist)
  real-time replay             : 585 late + 581 dropped of 59943 frames; 370/600 segments change
  (0.1+0.2)+0.3 = 0.6000000000000001   0.1+(0.2+0.3) = 0.6
  -> evidence/replay.json

=== STEP 2 · scenarios: mine the logs, build the grid, validate the simulator 
  worked example: gap 12 m, closing 5 m/s -> TTC = 2.40 s, required decel = 1.04 m/s^2
  mined 614 lead-braking events out of 9610 trajectories (6.4 %); peak lead decel p50 -2.5, p99 -4.2 m/s^2
  hardest braking any logged human applied (99.9th percentile): 3.6 m/s^2; the actuator can do 9
  grid 60 gaps x 45 closing speeds = 2700 cells; 9 % of the grid needs braking harder than any logged human ever did
  validation t_react=0.0: full-brake collides in 2.2 % of cells, physics says 2.2 % unavoidable; mismatching cells away from the frontier: 0
  validation t_react=0.6: full-brake collides in 7.4 % of cells, physics says 7.4 % unavoidable; mismatching cells away from the frontier: 0
  IDM, t_react 0.0 -> 0.6 s: collision area 2.2 % -> 7.4 %
  -> evidence/scenarios.json

